module contract;

// Fluent Bit direct-route array destructor — resource-projection vocabulary
// version 1.
//
// Card AOM-DESTROY-SIBLINGS. This module declares ONE TRUSTED ASSUMPTION about
// one C function. It verifies nothing: the C body is not analyzed by this
// loader, and a declaration is a premise. Any verdict that rests on this module
// is a verdict RELATIVE TO this declaration and is never a proof of the C
// implementation.
//
// The body was read at Fluent Bit `66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97`,
// `src/flb_input_chunk.c:2483-2504`, and is cited by line below. The C
// declaration in force is `include/fluent-bit/flb_input_chunk.h:162`:
//
//     void flb_input_chunk_destroy_direct_routes(struct flb_chunk_direct_route *routes,
//                                                int route_count);
//
// ## Read on its own terms, not on its neighbour's name
//
// Card ADDRESS-OF-MEMBER-DESTROY-UNDECLARED declared the sibling
// `flb_input_chunk_destroy` and deliberately left this function undeclared,
// naming `AOM-DESTROY-SIBLINGS-UNDECLARED` and warning that declaring either
// sibling on the strength of the other's shape would be the unfaithful
// declaration defect card P07 recorded. That warning is load-bearing here: the
// two contracts DISAGREE on the one conjunct that decides the outcome.
// `flb_input_chunk_destroy` is `nonnull`, because its first statement
// dereferences `ic`. THIS body's third statement is
//
//     if (!routes) {                                          // :2490
//         return;                                             // :2491
//     }
//
// so a null argument is defined behaviour here, and `nullable` is the faithful
// nullability — the same position `flb_free` holds, and for the same reason.
// Copying the sibling's `nonnull` would have been unfaithful in the
// RESTRICTIVE direction: it asserts a precondition the body does not have, and
// it would refuse every call site the projection cannot prove non-null while
// claiming the callee demanded that proof.
//
// ## Why this function is declared although the analysed unit defines it
//
// `flb_input_chunk_destroy_direct_routes` is defined in
// `src/flb_input_chunk.c`, itself a unit of the pinned corpus, so this is the
// arrangement cards PROD-CONTRACTS, NEIGHBOURS and
// ADDRESS-OF-MEMBER-DESTROY-UNDECLARED already established: a declared contract
// ALWAYS wins over a W4-inferred summary, a disagreement is reported as a
// `SummaryConflict` diagnostic and never silently resolved
// (`crates/qcore-c-gateway/src/legacy_summary.rs`), and declaring a defined
// function does not stop the unit analysing that function's own body — it only
// fixes what its CALLERS may assume. This function's own status is whatever its
// body earns, and this module does not move it.
//
// ## The custody statement, read against the body
//
// The body has exactly two exits, and `routes` is discharged on both.
//
//   * The guarded exit at `:2491` is reached only when `routes` is null. A null
//     pointer carries no caller obligation, so there is nothing to discharge
//     and nothing is leaked; `nullable` is exactly the mode that says so.
//   * On every other path control reaches
//
//         flb_free(routes);                                   // :2503
//
//     UNCONDITIONALLY. The only construct between `:2490` and `:2503` is one
//     `for` loop (`:2494-2501`) containing two `if` statements; the loop body
//     has no `break`, no `return` and no `goto`, and a non-positive
//     `route_count` simply skips it. There is no early exit anywhere in this
//     function other than the null guard.
//
// So the caller's obligation on `routes` is discharged in full on every path,
// which is what `resource_released` says. Over the whole v1 parameter
// vocabulary it is the only mode with that caller-side effect:
// `resource_borrowed` would leave a discharged obligation standing (which is
// precisely the leak family this declaration decides) and `resource_stored`
// would consume it under an owner that does not exist. An unfaithful
// declaration is worse than `Unknown`.
//
// `route_count` is an `int` by value. `resource_borrowed` is the only mode the
// loader admits on a scalar, and it is also the true one — the value bounds the
// member-release loop and selects nothing about the custody of `routes` itself.
//
// The result is `void`, so `resource_return_none()`.
//
// ## NOT CLAIMED, recorded rather than hidden
//
//   * **Nothing about the member releases** (`:2495-2500`). The loop frees
//     `routes[index].label` only when it is non-null AND
//     `routes[index].label_length > 0`, and `routes[index].plugin_name` only
//     when it is non-null. A non-null `label` recorded with a zero
//     `label_length` would therefore NOT be freed. In the producer this corpus
//     contains (`flb_input_chunk_get_direct_routes`) the two fields are written
//     together and cannot disagree, but that is a property of the producer, not
//     of this callee, and this declaration asserts nothing about either field.
//     The members are not the caller's `routes` obligation and this module does
//     not name them.
//   * **Nothing about `route_count` agreeing with the allocated extent.** If
//     `route_count` exceeds the number of elements `routes` was allocated for,
//     the loop reads out of bounds before the release. This module claims the
//     release HAPPENS; it does NOT claim it is SAFE. That is the same boundary
//     `fluent_bit/input_chunk_destroy.qcore` records for its own body.
//   * **Nothing about retention.** Whether any other object still holds
//     `routes`, or holds one of the member pointers this body frees, is the
//     unmodeled retention this corpus records as B03/R13 and is not decided
//     here.
//   * **`flb_input_chunk_destroy_corrupted` (`:2623-2701`) remains
//     DELIBERATELY UNDECLARED.** Card AOM-DESTROY-SIBLINGS read that body too
//     and wrote out the contract it would carry, and then measured its payoff
//     at ZERO: the pinned 185-unit corpus contains no
//     `call to uncontracted function \`flb_input_chunk_destroy_corrupted\``
//     leaf at all, because its single in-corpus call site
//     (`input_chunk_get`, `:2866`) is refused by the indirect-access rule
//     before the call rule is ever reached. Landing it would add an unpaid
//     premise. That is the same disposition, on the same rule, that card
//     CONSTRUCTOR-RESIDUALS-ABI-TYPEDEF gave `msgpack/packer.qcore`. Named
//     `AOM-DESTROY-CORRUPTED-UNPAID-PREMISE`.

extern fn flb_input_chunk_destroy_direct_routes(routes: ptr<mut, u8>, route_count: i32) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_released(routes, nullable);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(route_count);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
